WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.858
FAYE MOSS: Hello! Welcome to Superstars

2
00:00:04.859 --> 00:00:15.784
in History ! I'm your host, Faye Moss, and you're in for a royal treat today—another exclusive interview with a famous person from the

3
00:00:15.785 --> 00:00:21.691
past. Today's guest was a caring king who worked hard to improve life in his kingdom.

4
00:00:21.692 --> 00:00:30.184
In fact, he was so devoted to his people, he invented a brand-new alphabet for them. Straight

5
00:00:30.185 --> 00:00:52.027
from history, here is His Majesty King Sejong the Great! KING

6
00:00:52.028 --> 00:00:54.918
SEJONG: It pleases me to be here,

7
00:00:54.919 --> 00:00:58.408
Faye. FAYE MOSS: The honor is all mine,

8
00:00:58.409 --> 00:01:05.952
Your Majesty. I'm so excited to interview the most beloved king from the Joseon Dynasty

9
00:01:05.953 --> 00:01:15.843
of Korea. But I have to say, the surprising thing is that you became a king at all, since you were the third son of your father, King Taejong.

10
00:01:15.844 --> 00:01:19.082
How did you end up on the throne?

11
00:01:19.083 --> 00:01:24.666
KING SEJONG: As a child I was studious and serious, and my father looked kindly upon

12
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:33.730
this. Meanwhile my oldest brother, Prince Yangnyeong, who was the heir to the throne, was carefree and lacked the temperament required of a king.

13
00:01:33.731 --> 00:01:42.948
In 1418 the court officials were so exasperated by his behavior, they appealed to my father, saying the prince was unfit to rule. At

14
00:01:42.949 --> 00:01:49.709
the urging of these officials, my father stripped Prince Yangnyeong of the title crown prince and named me the heir instead. Two months

15
00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:54.785
later my father abdicated, and the throne passed to me. FAYE MOSS:

16
00:01:54.786 --> 00:02:02.458
You were only 21 when you became king. That must have been a huge responsibility! KING

17
00:02:02.459 --> 00:02:05.534
SEJONG: It was, and it happened very

18
00:02:05.535 --> 00:02:10.776
suddenly. I did not feel ready to become king, but my father gave me no choice.

19
00:02:10.777 --> 00:02:18.202
After his abdication he stayed involved in state affairs and daily governance for a few years to make the transition smooth. In

20
00:02:18.203 --> 00:02:24.115
1422 he fell ill and died, and from then on, I ruled alone. FAYE

21
00:02:24.116 --> 00:02:28.015
MOSS: Can you tell us about some of your accomplishments as king?

22
00:02:28.016 --> 00:02:35.979
KING SEJONG: Certainly, Faye. I'm sure you want to know about my alphabet, but even before I invented it, I had a deep interest in

23
00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:40.752
learning. As a child I loved to read and often read all through the night!

24
00:02:40.753 --> 00:02:45.561
To promote learning I established the Hall of Worthies early in my reign.

25
00:02:45.562 --> 00:02:53.521
This was a group of scholars whom I chose to advise me on policy, and research ways to improve the lives of my people. I

26
00:02:53.522 --> 00:03:00.209
recruited scholars from all backgrounds since their abilities were more important to me than their social class. FAYE MOSS:

27
00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:07.514
Your reign saw advances in science and technology—a lot like the Renaissance! KING

28
00:03:07.515 --> 00:03:12.547
SEJONG: I do not know what "the Renaissance" is, young lady, but you are

29
00:03:12.548 --> 00:03:17.783
correct. I believed that science and technology would help to strengthen our nation.

30
00:03:17.784 --> 00:03:23.507
A talented inventor named Jang Yeongsil invented a great many things at my request.

31
00:03:23.508 --> 00:03:27.870
He built water clocks, astrolabes, and sundials. He

32
00:03:27.871 --> 00:03:33.941
also made a rain gauge and a water gauge that allowed us to better manage our water supply. And he

33
00:03:33.942 --> 00:03:39.411
improved the metal printing press so that books could be printed even faster. FAYE MOSS:

34
00:03:39.412 --> 00:03:45.723
Now that you've mentioned books, let's move on to your alphabet, Hangeul! KING

35
00:03:45.724 --> 00:03:54.607
SEJONG: Hangeul? No, no. The name for my writing system is Hunminjeongeum , which means "correct sounds for the instruction of

36
00:03:54.608 --> 00:04:01.349
people." Back in my time, our language had no writing system of its own—we wrote with Chinese

37
00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:10.193
characters. But Chinese characters did not represent our speech accurately because our language was very different from Chinese.

38
00:04:10.194 --> 00:04:16.919
And learning Chinese characters was challenging; only elite men had time for this kind of study. I

39
00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:25.991
was saddened by the fact that so many of my subjects were trapped in illiteracy with few opportunities to acquire new knowledge. With some

40
00:04:25.992 --> 00:04:32.718
help from my children, I invented a new writing system that would be easy for everyone to learn. My creation was

41
00:04:32.719 --> 00:04:37.331
released to the public in 1446. FAYE MOSS: Your

42
00:04:37.332 --> 00:04:42.040
alphabet is unique among the world's writing systems. The shapes

43
00:04:42.041 --> 00:04:46.047
of the letters come from the sounds they make. KING SEJONG:

44
00:04:46.048 --> 00:04:52.950
You are a very bright young lady! You would make an excellent addition to the Hall of Worthies. Indeed,

45
00:04:52.951 --> 00:05:01.282
the shape of each consonant is inspired by the shape or position of the speech organs when making its sound. For example,

46
00:05:01.283 --> 00:05:09.260
nieun , the consonant for n , is shaped like the tongue when making a n sound. And

47
00:05:09.261 --> 00:05:16.590
mieum , which is for m , is meant to look like the lips when making a m

48
00:05:16.591 --> 00:05:21.702
sound. FAYE MOSS: I bet everyone was thrilled to have such an

49
00:05:21.703 --> 00:05:29.209
easy-to-learn alphabet! KING SEJONG: Hardly! Many nobles and elites at court enjoyed the power of being literate while the common

50
00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:39.548
people were not. So my invention was met with much opposition. I tirelessly promoted the alphabet, but I’m sorry to report it was slow

51
00:05:39.549 --> 00:05:45.119
to spread. Literacy was only one of the areas where I tried to improve the lives of

52
00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:49.240
my people. I made the tax system fairer for the common

53
00:05:49.241 --> 00:05:55.576
people. I had a textbook on farming published to help farmers succeed in producing food for the kingdom.

54
00:05:55.577 --> 00:06:02.813
I even enacted laws to give government slave women maternity leave and their husbands paternity leave.

55
00:06:02.814 --> 00:06:06.462
FAYE MOSS: It sounds like you were really ahead of your

56
00:06:06.463 --> 00:06:12.334
time. Some people think it’s easy to be a king. But you worked really

57
00:06:12.335 --> 00:06:16.321
hard! KING SEJONG: It was a very hard job—and not a

58
00:06:16.322 --> 00:06:23.222
healthy one. As king, I was constantly reading documents, often in poor lighting, which weakened my

59
00:06:23.223 --> 00:06:29.516
eyesight. I was served delicious, rich food but had little opportunity for

60
00:06:29.517 --> 00:06:33.793
exercise. Needless to say, I wasn't always in the best of health.

61
00:06:33.794 --> 00:06:41.305
But as king, I felt a duty to my people, and I'm proud to have improved their lives where I could. FAYE

62
00:06:41.306 --> 00:06:48.185
MOSS: With all that you accomplished, no one can accuse you of slacking off on the

63
00:06:48.186 --> 00:06:54.809
job! Thank you for sharing your story with us, Your Majesty. We'll let you get back to history now.

64
00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:59.525
KING

65
00:06:59.526 --> 00:07:10.082
SEJONG: Good-bye! FAYE MOSS: King Sejong spent the last third of his life suffering from blindness and diabetes, and his son and heir, Prince Munjong, ruled as

66
00:07:10.083 --> 00:07:16.290
regent for him. King Sejong died in 1450, after 32 years on

67
00:07:16.291 --> 00:07:22.796
the throne. His reign is remembered for its advances in science, technology,

68
00:07:22.797 --> 00:07:31.826
and literacy. In fact, October 9 is a national holiday in Korea, Hangeul Day, celebrating the day that Hunminjeongeum was

69
00:07:31.827 --> 00:07:40.882
published. And King Sejong's alphabet is still so admired that one of UNESCO's international literacy prizes is named after him.

70
00:07:40.883 --> 00:07:43.567
I hope you enjoyed today's guest.

71
00:07:43.568 --> 00:07:54.789
Be sure to tune in again next time for another blast from the past on Superstars in History !

